<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Nanzhao</h1><p class="st-meta">738 CE – 902 CE · capital Taihe, Yangjumie</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom around Lake Erhai in Yunnan that played Tang China and the Tibetan Empire against each other and dominated south-western trade routes for 160 years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Six chiefdoms (zhao) lived around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-erhai">Lake Erhai</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>. With <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> backing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piluoge">Piluoge</a>, ruler of the southernmost, Mengshe, united them by 738 and was recognised as Prince of Yunnan. He built his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taihe-nanzhao">Taihe</a>, and his successors moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangjumie">Yangjumie</a>, near modern Dali.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Meng kings ruled a mixed population, including ancestors of the Bai and Yi peoples, through Tang-style ministries and military districts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geluofeng">Geluofeng</a> broke with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> and destroyed Chinese armies in 751 and 754 as an ally of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yimouxun">Yimouxun</a> returned to Tang allegiance in 794. Nanzhao sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> in 829 and raided <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-general-to-pacify-the-south">Annan</a> in the 860s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> grew strongly at court, and Chinese writing was used for administration.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Costly wars weakened the monarchy. In 902 the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-maisi">Zheng Maisi</a> massacred the Meng royal family and founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/da-changhe">Da Changhe</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-erhai">Erhai</a> basin remained a political centre and passed in time to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dali-kingdom">Dali Kingdom</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nanzhao.jpg" alt="Nanzhao" width="320" height="429"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nam_Chi%E1%BA%BFu.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>738 CE – 902 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Taihe, Yangjumie</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Piluoge, Geluofeng, Yimouxun</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, local religions</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bai and Yi languages, Middle Chinese for writing</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a>, <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>, <a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nanzhao Kingdom, Mengshe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=820&amp;focus=nanzhao" aria-label="Nanzhao on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7334 1234 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nanzhao lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7654 1490L7372 1351M7654 1490L7771 1192M7654 1490L7565 1604"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7372 1351h0M7771 1192h0M7565 1604h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7654 1490h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7654 1490h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=820&amp;focus=nanzhao">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
